today we are going to discuss about  mutability data analysis and then get a  hands-on practice on t bioinfo server  for the same  so what is mutability mutability in  simple terms describes the observed rate  of a given position to change or mutate  the mutability module generates a table  in which each genomic position or a  nucleotide is assigned with the most  probable nucleotide variant  the variant is assigned based on the  maximum of the low thresholds of  confidence intervals  transformed to quasi-normal distribution  for each alternative in the given  position  so in the omics logic learn lesson on  mutability we discussed about mutation s  ps and mutability  now let's learn how we can use this  information to analyze ngs data from  different sources locations and ears to  derive by logical meaning regarding the  data  in the next steps we are going to learn  how we can perform mutability data  analysis on ncs data using tbioinfo  server  we will also go through the complete  pipeline for mutability analysis  and explain it stepwise eventually you  will learn about the interpretation of  the results as well  to build a mutability pipeline choose  mutation variant analysis pipeline under  the genomics section on the t bioinfo  server  now let's set the parameters  for type of reference genome it should  be our genome gtf since here we are not  using a model organism we need to  provide reference genome for desired  organism  that is plasmodium falciparum in our  example  the format of ngs data needs to be fastq  then we need to choose single or pair  end reads here we are going to choose  pair end since in this example we have  paired entry data  to upload the ngs data  the input data can be uploaded by using  multiple options files can be uploaded  as ftp or svl links they could also be  uploaded as ncbi run table  or as dot txt files containing svl or  ftp links for all the samples  next we need to upload the reference  genome of selected organism here we are  using plasmodium falciparum click on add  more option to create get link option  subsequently paste links for faster  files of reference genome and gtf or gff  file for reference to norm  next choose the groups drag the samples  into respective groups  here we are considering 2009 samples as  control groups and 2015 samples as  treatment groups  now let's start to build the pipeline  to begin with click on the start button  you will get a pop-up click on ok to  start the pipeline  to align the raw sequence reads of ngs  data here we are using the boutique  algorithm  by clicking on the bounty option you  will get a pop-up and click on save to  proceed further  next we need to perform per nuclear  mutation analysis  pernucle is an algorithm that calculates  the per position coverage of the  reference genome by all variants  the output of this module is a table  with all the positions of the genome and  the calculated coverages of each  position by each variant  next we need to perform  bionom  95 mutation called bionom 95 is a module  that performs calculation of the 95  percent confidence interval for  frequency of every variant at the genome  position based on wilkes theorem for  binomial distribution  to learn more about this module you can  refer to the learn lesson on genomics on  omics logic learn portal  next we need to perform mutability  the mutability module generates a table  in which each genomic position on  nucleotide is assigned to the most  probable nucleotide variant the variant  is assigned based on the maximum of the  low thresholds of confidence intervals  for each alternative in the given  position  the last step is to end the pipeline and  give it a name  this pipeline can take more than 72  hours to complete or more than that  depending on the number of samples  uploaded please keep patience when  looking at the results  okay so now we have our results  in the mutability table  each genomic position or nucleotide is  assigned to the most probable nucleotide  variant  and the variant is assigned based on the  maximum low thresholds of confidence  intervals  usually this table is very large  sometimes difficult to handle on the pc  systems  in order to understand and visualize  whether the mutation or mutation  frequency information can help us  distinguish various classes of the  samples in our data  we can use mason idea to understand and  visualize the patterns within our data  based on new features or principal  components  since the mutability output files are  usually very large to handle especially  if you have large number of samples that  would be more than 20 samples let's say  so it's better to not download the  complete file instead we can use the svl  link of the file  to do so first we need to go to the  download outputs of the mutability  pipeline next click on download job  temporary files  subsequently need to click on svls where  you will get  heterozygosity transform folder  in this folder you will get all  files.svl  open this file in dot txt format  and copy the full link of the file named  as heterozygosity  log  transform.txt now to run pca go to the t  bioinfo server  click on the unsupervised analysis  module under the data mining section  although there are number of options  available to upload file on the server  since mutability output files are  usually very large to handle specially  if you have large number of samples it's  better to use the svl link of the file  paste the link of the file in the blank  box  and run pca  to run pca click on the start button  and now click on pcr library  now end the pipeline give it a name  and run on cluster  in the output folder you will get two  important outputs  one is the pca plot in the pdf format  and second as a pca result table  in the pca table we can see pc1 pc2 and  pc3 that represents  the percentage variance of the data  you can also draw the scatter plots  based on these principal components  using excel  if you open another output you will find  the three pca plots that is pc1 versus  pc2 pc1 versus pc3 and pc2 versus pc3  two of which are shown here  from the pc plots based on the  mutability results we can clearly see  samples from 2009 and 2015 from clear  distant groups  further one of the samples from 2015  looks like an outlier this sample can be  removed from downstream analysis  this analysis indicates strains of p  falsely param that can be separated from  each other based on mutation frequency  information  thus it would be interesting to know  what are the important variants and  mutations in p falciparum between these  two ears  to learn more about the mutability  analysis and how do you interpret the  results  you can go to  the course number 10 on mutability  analysis and interpretation  under genomics section on omics logic  learn portal  for any questions you can reach out to  us directly at marketing at  omixlogic.com  and we'll be glad to assist you with  your questions
 

